1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

1
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:51,040
The pains have stopped. Nothing will
happen yet.

2
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,880
Everything is fine.
(HE SIGHS WITH RELIEF)

3
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:04,280
You mean it was a false alarm? Not
exactly.

4
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:07,880
These early labour pains show that
the womb is preparing itself for
birth.

5
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:11,480
Dr Clarkson, I'm afraid Lord Grantham
doesn't enjoy medical detail.

6
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:13,600
The point is, can we all go back to
bed?

7
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,120
You can.

8
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,240
And so can I. I'll see you out.

9
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,160
Sir Philip Tapsell will be here
tomorrow. Of course.

10
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:22,920
If you think it advisable.

11
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,800
There really is nothing wrong?
Nothing at all.

12
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:32,440
She a healthy young woman going
through a very normal and natural
process.

13
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:36,480
I think I'd rather be in the city if
I were having a baby.

14
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,760
Where they've got all the modern
inventions.

15
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,720
Far away from everyone you know and
trust? I don't think I would.

16
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:44,200
What are you talking about having
babies for, Ivy?

17
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,040
I think we can leave that for a
little further down the menu.

18
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,080
It's always an idea to be prepared.

19
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:53,040
I expect you're always prepared? I
try to be, Mr Barrow.

20
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:56,920
I don't like the direction this
conversation is taking.

21
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:00,000
Could we all begin the day's tasks,
please?

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,520
And remember - Lady Sybil is in a
delicate condition, so no noise on
the gallery.

23
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,960
It's exciting, though, in't it, to
have a baby in the house.

24
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,920
It won't make much difference to
you. Now get back in the kitchen,
and do as you're told!

25
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,120
Well, I think that message got
through.

26
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:23,760
We can't risk her welfare to soothe
Clarkson's feelings.

27
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:27,200
I know. I like the old boy, but he
did misdiagnose Matthew,

28
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,280
and he did miss the warning signs
with Lavinia.

29
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:32,320
Thank you, O'Brien.

30
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:33,920
Is that fair?

31
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,480
He didn't wanna get Matthew's hopes
up when it wouldn't make any
difference.

32
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,840
With Lavinia, the disease moved like
lightning.

33
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,920
I know, I know. But even so...

34
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:47,280
Sir Philip Tapsell may have delivered
many lords and royal highnesses,

35
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:49,320
but he doesn't know US.

36
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,400
I'll ask him to include Clarkson in
his deliberations. Will that satisfy
you?

37
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,400
I suppose so.

38
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:02,480
You look a bit puzzled. I am. Mr
Carson's asked me to wind the clocks.

39
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:07,440
You must be doing well. In this
house, that marks you out as First
Footman more than anything could.

40
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:12,040
That's just it. I said thank you
right away, but I know nothing about
clocks.

41
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:15,000
You'd better ask Mr Barrow. He's the
clock expert.

42
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,160
He used to wind them, but of course

43
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,280
it's quite wrong for a valet to do
it.

44
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:21,480
Mr Barrow won't mind? Oh, no.

45
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:25,760
I can see he likes you, and that's
good, since he's got the ear of His
Lordship.

46
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:28,120
Yes, I suppose he would have.

47
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:31,440
I'd keep in with him if I were you.
I will.

48
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,440
Thank you. Think nothing of it.

49
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:41,800
I'm the size of a house.

50
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,960
My back hurts,

51
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:46,960
my ankles are swelling,

52
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:49,080
and my head aches.

53
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,360
Honestly, I cannot recommend this to
anyone.

54
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,680
I am listening, but of course I'm
dying to start one of my own.

55
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:59,960
So, you're not waiting? Waiting for
what?

56
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:02,760
I don't know, but I did wonder.

57
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:07,960
Mary, you know what I said about the
baby being Catholic?

58
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:11,160
I've just realised that the
christening will have to be here.

59
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:12,760
In Downton.

60
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:13,920
Blimey.

61
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,160
I wanted the whole thing done in
Dublin.

62
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:18,160
Out of sight, out of mind.

63
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,680
But we can't wait forever.

64
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,040
We can't not christen it, the poor
thing.

65
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:27,560
You don't have to do this. It's your
baby too.

66
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:29,600
I don't mind.

67
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:36,160
I mean, I do believe in God, but all
the rest of it - vicars, feast days,

68
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,720
and deadly sins - I don't care about
all of that.

69
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,960
I don't know if a vicar knows any
more about God than I do.

70
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:49,680
And I love Tom so very, very much.

71
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:55,080
I'll let you rest.

72
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:00,480
And don't worry. I'll fight your
corner with Travis if it comes to
it.

73
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,920
There. You feel a slight increase in
the resistance?

74
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:14,600
I think so. That's what you're
watching for.

75
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:17,720
Never go past the point where the
clock is comfortable.

76
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,840
You make it sound like a living
thing.

77
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:22,560
Clocks are living things. My dad was
a clockmaker.

78
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:24,720
I grew up with clocks. I understand
them.

79
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:27,680
Never wind them in the early morning
before a room has warmed up,

80
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,040
nor too late, when the night air
cools them down.

81
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:32,640
Find the time when the family's out
of the room.

82
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:42,600
ANNA: But I don't understand. Why
was I kept away from you until now?

83
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:47,320
Whatever the reason, it's over. The
point is someone has to question Mrs
Bartlett.

84
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:51,080
You wrote and said she saw Vera on
the evening of the day of her death.

85
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,000
That's right.

86
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,120
She went for a walk. The door was
open, and she went in.

87
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:58,800
She saw Vera was cutting pastry from
under her nails.

88
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,960
I wrote that because it was such a
strange detail for her to remember.

89
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:06,320
She was making the pie that she ate
that night when I was on the train
back to Downton.

90
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,320
So, Vera planned this?

91
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:17,080
She meant for you to be imprisoned,
and hanged for her suicide?

92
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:18,920
It was her revenge.

93
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,960
And what a revenge, for both of us.

94
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:26,640
But they'll say you poisoned the
milk,

95
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:29,800
or the flour or something to catch
her after you'd gone.

96
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,400
They tested everything in the
kitchen. They said it was in the
pastry, where I couldn't have put it.

97
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:36,400
(SIGHS)

98
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:40,240
Oh, I hope she's burning in hell!

99
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,280
Don't go down that road.

100
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,280
Once you do, there's no way off it.

101
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:53,600
I've been thinking about you since
we last met,

102
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:56,040
and wanted to know how you were
living.

103
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:58,080
I've not gone back to...

104
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:01,240
..doing what I was doing, if that's
worrying you.

105
00:07:01,240 --> 00:07:04,440
I've no Charlie to feed, so now if I
starve, I starve alone.

106
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:07,680
I'd rather starve than do that.

107
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,040
You see...I thought you might work
here for a while.

108
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:14,080
Helping Mrs Bird.

109
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:17,960
It would mean that when you moved on
you will have had a respectable job

110
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:20,160
with a respectable reference.

111
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:22,800
Are you sure you've thought about
this, ma'am?

112
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,960
What will Mrs Hughes' reaction be, or
Lady Grantham's?

113
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:27,960
And old Lady Grantham?

114
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:30,600
I can't wait to hear what she has to
say about it.

115
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,600
Don't you want to come?

116
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:35,440
It's not that, ma'am.

117
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:39,320
You're offering a return to a
wholesome world, and I am very, very
grateful.

118
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:40,880
Well, then?

119
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:44,440
But I think it's going to be a lot
more complicated than you allow.

120
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:48,240
Then we shall have to face those
complications together, shan't we?

121
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:55,280
I'll take him from here. Sir.

122
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,080
Bates seems very cheerful. Is he?

123
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:02,440
He has a visit from his wife
earlier. She must have brought him
some good news.

124
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:04,440
It hardly seems fair, does it?

125
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:07,520
You've got an extra year, and I've
got a formal reprimand,

126
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:12,160
and Mr Bates, who's done it all,
who's made all the trouble for us,
gets good news.

127
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:14,160
What do you think it is?

128
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,520
She can't be pregnant. He was
arrested a year ago.

129
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:19,440
She might be, but he wouldn't be very
happy about it.

130
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,040
So, what do you wanna do? Um...

131
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:27,440
It'll need some thought. First, what
does this good news consist of?

132
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:31,320
When you shared with him, where did
he keep his letters?

133
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:37,600
Quite a few of the cottages have
been renovated.

134
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:39,920
Thanks to you. Maybe a little thanks
to me.

135
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:44,600
But many of the farms have been left
entirely to their own devices.

136
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,760
Coulter hasn't farmed this properly
for 20 years.

137
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:51,760
He struggles to pay the rent, which
is too low anyway.

138
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:54,520
There's been no...investment.

139
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:58,880
Papa would say you can't abandon
people just because they grow old.

140
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:01,600
I agree, but it would be cheaper to
give him a free cottage,

141
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:03,960
and work this land as it should be
worked.

142
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:07,520
I see. And you don't think Papa
understands that?

143
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:10,320
Maybe he harks back to a time when
money was abundant.

144
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:12,600
There wasn't much need to keep on
top of it.

145
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:15,720
I think he equates being
business-like with being mean.

146
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:18,240
Or worse, middle-class like me.

147
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:23,400
The middle classes have their
virtues, and husbandry is one.

148
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:29,440
We ought to get back. Sir Philip
thingy's due on the seven o'clock
train.

149
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:31,680
You ought to be there to hold Tom's
hand.

150
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,720
Poor fellow.

151
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:36,600
He's so terrified, and so thrilled
at the same time.

152
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,640
As I would be.

153
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:41,360
As I WILL be.

154
00:09:52,963 --> 00:09:57,403
The dear Duchess of Truro is full of
your praises, Sir Philip.

155
00:09:57,403 --> 00:09:59,443
Then of course, you know that.

156
00:09:59,443 --> 00:10:03,003
She had quite a time when she was
first married, but I said to her,

157
00:10:03,003 --> 00:10:06,643
"Never fear, Duchess, I'll get a
baby out of you one way or another."

158
00:10:08,723 --> 00:10:10,243
And so you did.

159
00:10:10,243 --> 00:10:15,723
Three boys, and as a result, a
secure dynasty, I'm glad to say.

160
00:10:15,723 --> 00:10:17,843
But you see no complications here?

161
00:10:17,843 --> 00:10:21,963
None at all. Lady Sybil is a perfect
model of health and beauty.

162
00:10:24,363 --> 00:10:29,203
We told our local doctor we'd send a
message to him when it looks as if
the baby's coming.

163
00:10:29,203 --> 00:10:32,163
Dr Clarkson has known us all since
we were girls.

164
00:10:32,923 --> 00:10:38,323
Yet what's needed here, Lady Mary, is
a knowledge of childbirth, nothing
more.

165
00:10:38,323 --> 00:10:42,003
But if it soothes you, then of
course.

166
00:10:42,843 --> 00:10:44,843
He's most welcome.

167
00:10:46,003 --> 00:10:48,083
I'm going to check on Sybil.

168
00:10:48,083 --> 00:10:51,963
Sorry to trouble you, My Lord, but I
wondered if I might have a word?

169
00:10:51,963 --> 00:10:53,723
Come into the library.

170
00:10:53,723 --> 00:10:56,723
Matthew, will you take Sir Philip to
the drawing room?

171
00:10:59,443 --> 00:11:01,163
Shall we go in?

172
00:11:01,163 --> 00:11:05,883
Sir Philip, I was rather hoping to
have you to myself for a moment.

173
00:11:06,883 --> 00:11:10,323
Do you know that I was injured
during the war?

174
00:11:10,323 --> 00:11:14,163
I think I did hear something about it
from Lady Grantham.

175
00:11:14,163 --> 00:11:16,843
My spine was very severely bruised,

176
00:11:17,843 --> 00:11:20,603
and for a time it seemed I'd lost
the use of my legs,

177
00:11:20,603 --> 00:11:22,043
and...

178
00:11:22,043 --> 00:11:23,643
everything else.

179
00:11:23,643 --> 00:11:28,283
But the bruising reduced, and you
recovered? I have heard of this.

180
00:11:28,283 --> 00:11:31,163
Well, how relieved you must have
been.

181
00:11:31,163 --> 00:11:36,643
Yes, but I wonder now whether the
injury might have...

182
00:11:36,643 --> 00:11:39,083
affected my...

183
00:11:41,723 --> 00:11:43,723
I suppose I mean my fertility.

184
00:11:43,723 --> 00:11:46,843
If it may have limited my chances of
fathering a child.

185
00:11:46,843 --> 00:11:50,923
Well, is...is everything working as
it should?

186
00:11:52,043 --> 00:11:53,243
Yes.

187
00:11:53,243 --> 00:11:55,123
Then um...

188
00:11:55,123 --> 00:11:57,403
why do you think there may be a
problem?

189
00:11:57,403 --> 00:12:00,283
We're anxious to start a family.

190
00:12:00,883 --> 00:12:04,883
We've been married for a few months
without any...results.

191
00:12:04,883 --> 00:12:06,923
My dear Mr Crawley,

192
00:12:06,923 --> 00:12:09,323
may I point out the word that gives
you away?

193
00:12:10,523 --> 00:12:12,043
Anxious.

194
00:12:12,043 --> 00:12:15,003
Anxiety is an enemy to pregnancy.

195
00:12:15,003 --> 00:12:18,843
Don't, whatever you do, feel anxious.

196
00:12:20,283 --> 00:12:24,443
I can run a test if you wish, but I
would urge you

197
00:12:24,443 --> 00:12:27,603
not to bother for some time yet.

198
00:12:29,403 --> 00:12:32,283
There you are. We were wondering
what had happened to you.

199
00:12:34,603 --> 00:12:36,603
This is extraordinary.

200
00:12:38,283 --> 00:12:40,563
Why did the police miss it so
completely?

201
00:12:41,003 --> 00:12:44,243
Mrs Bartlett never spoke to them.
She never spoke to anyone.

202
00:12:44,243 --> 00:12:45,563
Except to you.

203
00:12:45,563 --> 00:12:48,803
She didn't think the truth would
make any difference now.

204
00:12:48,803 --> 00:12:51,363
She thought it was only further
proof of his guilt.

205
00:12:51,363 --> 00:12:55,683
The difficulty is she may not want to
accept Bates' innocence.

206
00:12:55,683 --> 00:12:58,003
Doesn't she have to? Not
necessarily.

207
00:12:58,003 --> 00:13:02,003
She may think he drove his wife to
suicide, and deserves to rot in
prison.

208
00:13:02,003 --> 00:13:05,883
In short, she may not wish to tell
the truth to set him free.

209
00:13:07,283 --> 00:13:11,523
Then we need to get a statement from
her before she finds out it could
overturn the case.

210
00:13:11,523 --> 00:13:15,963
I'll telephone Murray tonight. He can
come up here and talk to you, and see
Bates.

211
00:13:15,963 --> 00:13:18,003
You were right, though.

212
00:13:18,003 --> 00:13:20,283
The proof was out there, and you
found it.

213
00:13:28,443 --> 00:13:31,923
The editor of The Sketch wants me to
write for him.

214
00:13:31,923 --> 00:13:35,923
He saw my letter to The Times, and
wants to give me a regular column.

215
00:13:37,483 --> 00:13:39,363
How regular?

216
00:13:39,363 --> 00:13:41,283
And what about?

217
00:13:41,283 --> 00:13:44,363
Once a week, and I can write about
whatever I like.

218
00:13:45,283 --> 00:13:51,003
It would be the problems faced by a
modern woman rather than the fall of
the Ottoman Empire, but even so.

219
00:13:51,003 --> 00:13:53,363
Will you write under your own name?

220
00:13:53,363 --> 00:13:55,163
I hadn't thought.

221
00:13:55,163 --> 00:13:59,483
You won't have an option. That's what
he's buying, that's what he wants -
your name and title.

222
00:14:01,203 --> 00:14:05,603
I don't know. I thought Edith's
letter to The Times was very
interesting.

223
00:14:05,603 --> 00:14:09,283
Don't bother, Matthew - I'm always a
failure in this family.

224
00:14:14,563 --> 00:14:16,563
I'm sad to hear this, Mrs Bird.

225
00:14:17,203 --> 00:14:21,003
And I'm sad to say it, madam, but
it's kept me awake all night,

226
00:14:21,003 --> 00:14:25,403
and I know I cannot work alongside a
woman of the...

227
00:14:26,723 --> 00:14:30,283
..a woman who has chosen that way of
life.

228
00:14:30,283 --> 00:14:32,003
But Miss Parks has changed.

229
00:14:32,003 --> 00:14:34,003
Maybe she has and maybe she hasn't.

230
00:14:34,643 --> 00:14:38,723
But if I tolerate her, I would be
tarnished by it.

231
00:14:38,723 --> 00:14:41,723
Suppose people come to think that I'd
followed the same...

232
00:14:41,723 --> 00:14:43,643
profession as what she has.

233
00:14:43,643 --> 00:14:47,603
Nobody could look at you and think
that, Mrs Bird.

234
00:14:47,603 --> 00:14:50,723
Well, I hope not, because I'm a
respectable woman.

235
00:14:51,803 --> 00:14:55,123
I may not have much, but I have my
good name,

236
00:14:55,123 --> 00:14:57,163
and I must protect it.

237
00:14:57,163 --> 00:14:59,723
You'll have a month's wages in lieu
of notice.

238
00:14:59,723 --> 00:15:03,363
Where will you go? Back to
Manchester.

239
00:15:03,363 --> 00:15:05,563
I can stay with my sister.

240
00:15:05,563 --> 00:15:09,123
She says there's plenty of work for a
plain cook these days.

241
00:15:09,123 --> 00:15:11,123
And they will find one in you.

242
00:15:11,923 --> 00:15:14,683
Goodbye, Mrs Bird, and good luck.

243
00:15:18,643 --> 00:15:21,363
Anything else you need to know about
having babies, Ivy?

244
00:15:21,363 --> 00:15:26,203
Honestly, if I told Mrs Patmore the
things you two say to me you'd be up
before Mr Carson.

245
00:15:26,203 --> 00:15:28,243
What are you doing with your
afternoon off?

246
00:15:28,243 --> 00:15:30,963
None of your business. I'd like to
make it my business.

247
00:15:32,283 --> 00:15:34,723
Have you greased the cake tins? Yes.

248
00:15:34,723 --> 00:15:36,323
What about the pastry?

249
00:15:36,323 --> 00:15:39,803
It's in the larder. Get started on
the vegetables for tonight.

250
00:15:42,483 --> 00:15:45,203
She doesn't want much, does she? She
doesn't like me.

251
00:15:45,203 --> 00:15:47,243
Why not? I don't know.

252
00:15:47,243 --> 00:15:48,803
She just doesn't.

253
00:15:48,803 --> 00:15:52,203
Well, anyone who doesn't like YOU
needs their head examining.

254
00:15:53,203 --> 00:15:55,283
I hope you agree with him, Jimmy?

255
00:15:55,283 --> 00:15:57,283
That'd be telling.

256
00:16:01,483 --> 00:16:03,483
Are we the first down?

257
00:16:03,963 --> 00:16:06,163
How is Sybil? Sleeping, thank God.

258
00:16:06,563 --> 00:16:10,363
She's been restless all afternoon.
I don't think it'll be long now.

259
00:16:10,363 --> 00:16:12,723
I'm sorry it couldn't have been in
Dublin.

260
00:16:12,723 --> 00:16:16,043
We know how much it meant. Nothing
means more than she does.

261
00:16:17,203 --> 00:16:19,963
And you're sure you have everything
you need? Quite sure.

262
00:16:19,963 --> 00:16:22,123
Hello, Granny. You're here. How nice.

263
00:16:22,123 --> 00:16:25,643
Your grandmother will be with us
every night until the baby's born.

264
00:16:25,643 --> 00:16:27,723
I hate to get news second-hand.

265
00:16:27,723 --> 00:16:29,723
Well, you won't have long to wait.

266
00:16:29,923 --> 00:16:32,723
I thought I'd bring up Dr Clarkson
after we've eaten.

267
00:16:32,723 --> 00:16:36,923
Yes, I've been talking to Lord
Grantham about the good doctor.

268
00:16:36,923 --> 00:16:39,563
Sir Philip feels the room would be
too crowded.

269
00:16:39,563 --> 00:16:42,923
It might be better to leave old
Clarkson out of it for the time
being.

270
00:16:42,923 --> 00:16:45,003
But I said I'd telephone.

271
00:16:45,003 --> 00:16:48,243
Well, it really isn't necessary.

272
00:16:48,243 --> 00:16:50,563
I've given him my word.

273
00:16:52,003 --> 00:16:55,243
Why don't I run down in the car
after dinner, and fetch him?

274
00:16:56,363 --> 00:17:00,883
The hollandaise for the fish, put it
in the sauce boats, and do the
souffles.

275
00:17:00,883 --> 00:17:03,803
As soon as - Will you just do it?!

276
00:17:05,603 --> 00:17:07,603
Out of my way, quick.

277
00:17:09,923 --> 00:17:11,323
What are you doing?

278
00:17:17,283 --> 00:17:20,443
Haven't you done it? Oh, my God.

279
00:17:20,443 --> 00:17:23,923
What's happened? It's curdled, and
it's got to go up in a minute. Oh,
my Lord!

280
00:17:23,923 --> 00:17:27,803
Ivy can manage it. Go on with what
you're doing. Can you really?

281
00:17:27,803 --> 00:17:29,803
I can.

282
00:17:30,443 --> 00:17:32,443
Now what? Give me an egg quick.

283
00:17:37,643 --> 00:17:40,563
Dribble it in. But it's ruined. Do
what I say.

284
00:17:44,163 --> 00:17:46,123
How does that work?

285
00:17:46,123 --> 00:17:48,883
It's magic. It's one of the tricks of
the trade.

286
00:17:52,483 --> 00:17:56,723
How have you done that? Just...one
of the tricks of the trade.

287
00:17:58,723 --> 00:18:02,723
Well, go and take it up. Well done,
Ivy. You played a good one there.

288
00:18:02,723 --> 00:18:06,083
Thank her, Daisy. Yes, thank you.

289
00:18:06,923 --> 00:18:08,963
That didn't hurt at all, did it?

290
00:18:11,003 --> 00:18:15,523
I'll tell you what, Daisy - Alfred
won't like you any better for being
rough on her.

291
00:18:17,363 --> 00:18:19,363
I should have the fish. I'll do it.

292
00:18:27,083 --> 00:18:31,403
(SIGHS) There's nothing more tiring
than waiting for something to happen.

293
00:18:33,363 --> 00:18:36,643
Edith, have you written back to your
editor yet?

294
00:18:36,643 --> 00:18:40,963
What's this? Edith has had an
invitation to write a newspaper
column.

295
00:18:43,363 --> 00:18:47,083
When may she expect an offer to
appear on the London stage?

296
00:18:47,083 --> 00:18:48,963
See?

297
00:18:52,563 --> 00:18:54,523
Oh, God, is it beginning?

298
00:18:59,123 --> 00:19:01,123
Dinner's suspended, so to speak.

299
00:19:02,723 --> 00:19:06,203
Yes, but suspended cancelled, or
suspended keeping it hot?

300
00:19:06,203 --> 00:19:08,203
What should I do about dinner down
here?

301
00:19:08,203 --> 00:19:10,243
I couldn't tell you.

302
00:19:11,563 --> 00:19:13,563
But...

303
00:19:14,843 --> 00:19:16,923
What do you mean, concerned?

304
00:19:16,923 --> 00:19:20,723
Lady Sybil's ankles are swollen. She
seems...muddled.

305
00:19:20,723 --> 00:19:24,923
What sort of muddled? Not quite
there, not quite in the present
moment.

306
00:19:24,923 --> 00:19:28,163
And what do you think it means? It
means she's having a baby.

307
00:19:29,643 --> 00:19:31,643
A word, Dr Clarkson.

308
00:19:32,643 --> 00:19:34,683
Excuse me.

309
00:19:38,363 --> 00:19:40,843
Sir Philip mustn't bully him into
silence.

310
00:19:40,843 --> 00:19:43,843
My dear, this is just Clarkson's
professional pride,

311
00:19:43,843 --> 00:19:46,363
like barber's asking who last cut
your hair.

312
00:19:46,363 --> 00:19:49,563
They always want to be better than
any other practitioner.

313
00:19:49,563 --> 00:19:51,643
But we must listen to what he has to
say.

314
00:19:51,643 --> 00:19:54,563
I quite agree. I don't want to hurt
Sir Philip's feelings.

315
00:19:54,563 --> 00:20:00,723
If there's one thing that I'm quite
indifferent to it's Sir Philip
Tapsell's feelings.

316
00:20:00,723 --> 00:20:03,203
You are upsetting these people for
no reason at all.

317
00:20:03,203 --> 00:20:05,083
I am not!

318
00:20:05,083 --> 00:20:08,123
I think she may be toxaemic, with a
danger of eclampsia.

319
00:20:08,123 --> 00:20:11,843
In which case, we must act fast.
There is no danger whatsoever.

320
00:20:11,843 --> 00:20:16,363
Judging by my experience, Lady Sybil
is behaving perfectly normally.

321
00:20:16,363 --> 00:20:20,163
Do you not find the baby small? Not
unusually so.

322
00:20:20,163 --> 00:20:21,723
And the ankles?

323
00:20:21,723 --> 00:20:24,483
Maybe she has thick ankles. Lots of
women do.

324
00:20:24,483 --> 00:20:25,763
But SHE does not.

325
00:20:26,723 --> 00:20:29,923
I'm warning you, Doctor, if you wish
to remain,

326
00:20:29,923 --> 00:20:32,123
you must be silent.

327
00:20:32,123 --> 00:20:34,123
I cannot allow you to interfere.

328
00:20:36,483 --> 00:20:38,203
(SIGHS)

329
00:20:44,317 --> 00:20:45,917
Oh!

330
00:20:48,517 --> 00:20:50,517
Don't be burnt...

331
00:20:52,557 --> 00:20:54,557
(SCREAMS)

332
00:21:03,317 --> 00:21:06,477
Are you all right, Ethel? Only, I
heard a shout.

333
00:21:06,477 --> 00:21:08,677
Fine, ma'am. Everything's fine.

334
00:21:10,197 --> 00:21:12,197
It's a kidney souffle, ma'am.

335
00:21:13,117 --> 00:21:15,797
A kidney souffle? Isn't that a bit
adventurous?

336
00:21:15,797 --> 00:21:18,797
I've seen Mrs Patmore do it a hundred
times.

337
00:21:18,797 --> 00:21:23,917
Yes, but she can't have begun her
career as a cook by making a kidney
souffle.

338
00:21:26,117 --> 00:21:28,157
Shall I try something else, ma'am?

339
00:21:28,157 --> 00:21:31,077
No, if we're to avoid a midnight
feast, it's...

340
00:21:33,197 --> 00:21:35,197
..too late to turn back.

341
00:21:40,957 --> 00:21:42,797
Is everything all right?

342
00:21:42,797 --> 00:21:46,717
I think so. I've just come to fetch
some warm milk in case she fancies
it.

343
00:21:47,877 --> 00:21:49,917
Mr Carson?

344
00:21:49,917 --> 00:21:51,917
I'm glad I've caught you.

345
00:21:53,197 --> 00:21:57,797
I've had a letter from Mrs Bird
who used to work for Mrs Crawley.

346
00:21:57,797 --> 00:22:00,917
I didn't know she'd gone. Well,
that's the point.

347
00:22:08,757 --> 00:22:11,197
I've been thinking about what we
should do.

348
00:22:11,197 --> 00:22:15,357
You know I have a brother in
Liverpool, and there might be an
opening there.

349
00:22:15,357 --> 00:22:17,557
It'd mean working with cars again -
No.

350
00:22:19,117 --> 00:22:22,477
We're not going backwards. You must
promise me that.

351
00:22:24,117 --> 00:22:25,357
(GROANS)

352
00:22:25,357 --> 00:22:27,437
God, I wish there was something I
could do.

353
00:22:28,557 --> 00:22:30,917
Just be here.

354
00:22:30,917 --> 00:22:33,157
We can just lie back and look at the
stars.

355
00:22:33,157 --> 00:22:34,517
(GASPS)

356
00:22:34,517 --> 00:22:35,877
Is she...?

357
00:22:35,877 --> 00:22:37,877
It's all just as it should be.

358
00:22:38,517 --> 00:22:40,557
Now what?

359
00:22:40,557 --> 00:22:44,317
I want to test the latest sample of
her urine. Oh, for Heaven's sake.

360
00:22:44,317 --> 00:22:47,757
Just give the order to the nurse,
please, Sir Philip.

361
00:22:52,837 --> 00:22:55,837
How's the young mother doing? Am I
on duty, Dr Clarkson?

362
00:22:55,837 --> 00:23:00,757
What? Only I swear I'm not on duty,
otherwise I wouldn't be lying here.

363
00:23:02,997 --> 00:23:05,997
No. No, you're not on duty.

364
00:23:05,997 --> 00:23:08,117
(SHE MOANS)

365
00:23:08,117 --> 00:23:10,117
(HYPERVENTILATES)

366
00:23:14,877 --> 00:23:17,957
Mrs Crawley has hired a prostitute to
manage her house.

367
00:23:17,957 --> 00:23:22,637
And that's why Mrs Bird felt she had
no choice but to hand in her notice.

368
00:23:22,637 --> 00:23:27,597
Nor did she, poor woman. Mr Carson,
this is Ethel we're talking about.

369
00:23:27,597 --> 00:23:29,637
OUR Ethel.

370
00:23:29,637 --> 00:23:34,157
And Mrs Crawley was just trying to
give her a helping hand. Is that so
wrong?

371
00:23:34,157 --> 00:23:38,517
I do not criticise her for her
charity, but she hasn't considered
her actions.

372
00:23:38,517 --> 00:23:41,757
No respectable person - certainly, no
respectable woman -

373
00:23:41,757 --> 00:23:43,797
can now be seen entering her house.

374
00:23:43,797 --> 00:23:46,037
But Ethel's given all that up.

375
00:23:46,037 --> 00:23:50,357
I didn't think she was running a
brothel in Mrs Crawley's kitchen.

376
00:23:50,357 --> 00:23:52,797
Can't we say nothing for now?

377
00:23:52,797 --> 00:23:56,077
Mrs Bird's gone, and I don't
remember Ethel as any great cook,

378
00:23:56,077 --> 00:23:58,117
so it may sort itself out.

379
00:23:58,117 --> 00:23:59,357
(SIGHS)

380
00:23:59,357 --> 00:24:01,397
Very well.

381
00:24:01,397 --> 00:24:04,557
We shall keep silent. For the moment.

382
00:24:04,557 --> 00:24:09,317
But I don't want the maids going into
that house on any pretext whatsoever.

383
00:24:09,317 --> 00:24:11,357
Is that clear?

384
00:24:11,357 --> 00:24:13,357
Quite clear, Mr Carson.

385
00:24:14,277 --> 00:24:16,277
Or the footmen.

386
00:24:20,797 --> 00:24:23,477
It's my belief that Lady Sybil is at
risk of eclampsia.

387
00:24:23,477 --> 00:24:28,237
What is that? A rare condition from
which she is NOT suffering.

388
00:24:28,237 --> 00:24:30,277
Tell him why you think she may be.

389
00:24:30,277 --> 00:24:34,997
Her baby is small, she is confused,
and there is far too much albumen -

390
00:24:34,997 --> 00:24:37,037
that is, protein - in her urine.

391
00:24:37,037 --> 00:24:40,597
Dr Clarkson, please! Have you
forgotten my mother is present?

392
00:24:40,597 --> 00:24:45,477
Please. A woman of my age can face
reality far better than most men.

393
00:24:45,477 --> 00:24:50,197
The fact remains, if I am right, we
must act at once. And do what?

394
00:24:50,197 --> 00:24:54,197
Get her down to the hospital, and
deliver the child by Caesarean
section.

395
00:24:54,197 --> 00:24:57,437
But is that safe? It is the opposite
of safe.

396
00:24:57,437 --> 00:24:59,877
It would expose mother and child to
untold danger.

397
00:24:59,877 --> 00:25:02,677
She could pick up any kind of
infection in a public hospital.

398
00:25:02,677 --> 00:25:08,117
An immediate delivery is the only
chance of avoiding the fits brought
on by the trauma of natural birth!

399
00:25:09,677 --> 00:25:12,677
It may not work, but - Honesty at
last.

400
00:25:12,677 --> 00:25:16,157
Even if she were at risk from
eclampsia, which she is NOT,

401
00:25:16,997 --> 00:25:22,037
a caesarean is a gamble which might
kill either or both of them.

402
00:25:22,037 --> 00:25:24,237
I think we must support Sir Philip
in this.

403
00:25:24,237 --> 00:25:26,277
But it's not our decision.

404
00:25:26,277 --> 00:25:27,957
What does Tom say?

405
00:25:27,957 --> 00:25:29,997
Tom has not hired Sir Philip.

406
00:25:29,997 --> 00:25:33,437
He is not master here, and I will
not put Sybil at risk on a whim.

407
00:25:33,437 --> 00:25:37,997
If you are sure, Sir Philip? I am
quite, quite certain.

408
00:25:37,997 --> 00:25:40,757
You're being ridiculous. Obviously,
we have to talk to Tom.

409
00:25:42,157 --> 00:25:44,157
Don't look at me. Cora is right.

410
00:25:45,197 --> 00:25:47,317
The decision lies with the chauffeur.

411
00:25:51,197 --> 00:25:53,957
How are things going? I'm not sure.

412
00:25:53,957 --> 00:25:56,797
The doctors are arguing. That's never
a good sign.

413
00:25:56,797 --> 00:26:01,077
Is everything all right?
Unfortunately, it seems it is not.

414
00:26:03,677 --> 00:26:06,877
Could we get her to hospital? To
move her would be tantamount to
murder.

415
00:26:06,877 --> 00:26:10,117
Sir Philip, admit you're beginning
to detect the symptoms yourself.

416
00:26:10,117 --> 00:26:14,477
You can see her distress! Can you?
Yes, Lady Sybil is in distress.

417
00:26:14,477 --> 00:26:16,517
She's about to give birth.

418
00:26:16,517 --> 00:26:19,237
Lord Grantham, Mr Branson, time is
running out.

419
00:26:19,237 --> 00:26:22,637
We'd be at the hospital by now if
we'd acted at once. The baby would be
born.

420
00:26:22,637 --> 00:26:25,837
If she has the operation now, do you
swear you can save her?

421
00:26:25,837 --> 00:26:27,837
I cannot swear it, no.

422
00:26:28,837 --> 00:26:32,437
But if we do not operate, and I am
right about her condition, then she
will die.

423
00:26:32,437 --> 00:26:34,797
If, if! Lord Grantham, can you
please take command?

424
00:26:34,797 --> 00:26:37,037
Tom, Dr Clarkson is not sure he can
save her.

425
00:26:37,037 --> 00:26:40,797
Sir Philip is certain he can bring
her through it with a living child.

426
00:26:40,797 --> 00:26:43,357
Isn't a certainty stronger than a
doubt?

427
00:26:43,357 --> 00:26:46,437
I don't mean to insult Sir Philip,
but Dr Clarkson knows Sybil.

428
00:26:46,437 --> 00:26:49,317
He's known her all her life. So
you'd take her to the hospital?

429
00:26:49,317 --> 00:26:52,397
I would've taken her an hour ago!
(SCREAMING)

430
00:26:52,397 --> 00:26:54,197
God help us!

431
00:26:56,517 --> 00:26:58,517
(SCREAMING CONTINUES)

432
00:27:05,997 --> 00:27:08,037
Any news from the house, ma'am?

433
00:27:08,037 --> 00:27:10,077
Not yet.

434
00:27:10,077 --> 00:27:13,397
Matthew said he would try and
telephone if it's not too late.

435
00:27:14,197 --> 00:27:16,237
Lady Sybil was always kind to me.

436
00:27:16,237 --> 00:27:18,237
Yes, she's a very dear girl.

437
00:27:21,117 --> 00:27:22,477
What...

438
00:27:22,477 --> 00:27:23,757
What's in this?

439
00:27:24,757 --> 00:27:26,757
Some honey. Was that not right?

440
00:27:28,957 --> 00:27:30,957
It's perfectly fine for now, Ethel,

441
00:27:31,957 --> 00:27:33,997
but perhaps not another time.

442
00:27:36,117 --> 00:27:38,157
Would you like anything, Mama? No.

443
00:27:38,157 --> 00:27:40,997
Just good news of the baby, and a
car to take me home.

444
00:27:40,997 --> 00:27:43,597
I don't suppose I shall get either
before long.

445
00:27:45,397 --> 00:27:47,437
What about you, Tom?

446
00:27:47,437 --> 00:27:49,477
I just feel so helpless.

447
00:27:49,477 --> 00:27:52,557
We men are always helpless when a
baby's in the picture.

448
00:27:52,837 --> 00:27:54,837
(DOOR OPENS)

449
00:27:56,277 --> 00:27:59,037
You can come up. It's a girl.

450
00:27:59,037 --> 00:28:01,397
And they're both... They're fine.

451
00:28:03,637 --> 00:28:06,037
Oh, thank God.

452
00:28:06,037 --> 00:28:08,037
And hallelujah!

453
00:28:13,117 --> 00:28:15,077
She's so beautiful.

454
00:28:16,157 --> 00:28:18,157
Oh, my darling,

455
00:28:18,717 --> 00:28:20,717
I do love you so much.

456
00:28:26,077 --> 00:28:28,077
I just want to sleep, really.

457
00:28:29,237 --> 00:28:31,277
Of course you do. You've earned it.

458
00:28:31,277 --> 00:28:33,277
She's a wonderful baby.

459
00:28:35,557 --> 00:28:37,557
I think we should let her sleep.

460
00:28:43,597 --> 00:28:45,597
Very well done. Thank you.

461
00:28:51,517 --> 00:28:53,517
Mama?

462
00:28:54,037 --> 00:28:56,037
Yes, my darling?

463
00:28:56,757 --> 00:29:00,117
Tom is thinking of getting a job in
Liverpool,

464
00:29:00,117 --> 00:29:03,637
going back to being a mechanic, but
it wouldn't be right for him.

465
00:29:04,277 --> 00:29:06,317
He needs to move forward.

466
00:29:06,317 --> 00:29:08,357
We'll talk about it tomorrow.

467
00:29:08,357 --> 00:29:10,397
You don't need to worry about it now.

468
00:29:10,397 --> 00:29:11,997
I think...

469
00:29:11,997 --> 00:29:14,077
Papa may see it as some kind of
answer,

470
00:29:14,557 --> 00:29:15,757
and -

471
00:29:15,757 --> 00:29:17,797
Your father loves you very much.

472
00:29:17,797 --> 00:29:19,797
I know.

473
00:29:20,517 --> 00:29:22,517
I know, and I love him terribly.

474
00:29:24,197 --> 00:29:28,237
But will you help me do battle for
Tom and the baby if the time comes?

475
00:29:28,237 --> 00:29:30,237
Of course. Lady Grantham?

476
00:29:31,637 --> 00:29:33,637
Now sleep, darling.

477
00:29:51,597 --> 00:29:53,957
I'm sorry we doubted. No.

478
00:29:53,957 --> 00:29:56,717
As to that, Lady Grantham, it's
always a good idea

479
00:29:56,717 --> 00:29:59,797
to forget most of what was said
during the waiting time,

480
00:29:59,797 --> 00:30:01,797
and simply enjoy the result.

481
00:30:02,557 --> 00:30:04,597
Is there anything more to be done?

482
00:30:04,597 --> 00:30:05,917
Not really.

483
00:30:05,917 --> 00:30:10,117
The nurse will stay with her, and so
I suggest we all get some sleep,

484
00:30:10,117 --> 00:30:12,437
and meet again refreshed in the
morning.

485
00:30:12,437 --> 00:30:14,477
Show us a card trick, Jimmy.

486
00:30:14,477 --> 00:30:16,517
It's your move.

487
00:30:16,517 --> 00:30:19,277
That's it. The baby is born.

488
00:30:19,717 --> 00:30:21,757
It's a girl.

489
00:30:21,757 --> 00:30:23,917
Fantastic. Now you can all go to
bed.

490
00:30:28,397 --> 00:30:31,597
Good news. Do you like Lady Sybil?

491
00:30:31,597 --> 00:30:34,717
I do. We worked together in the
hospital during the war,

492
00:30:34,717 --> 00:30:37,237
so I know her better than all of
them, really.

493
00:30:37,237 --> 00:30:39,557
She's a lovely person, like you.

494
00:30:49,037 --> 00:30:51,077
Anything the matter? No.

495
00:30:51,077 --> 00:30:54,397
No, but Mr Barrow's so familiar all
the time, isn't he?

496
00:30:55,437 --> 00:30:57,997
I'm glad to hear it. That's a very
good sign.

497
00:30:57,997 --> 00:31:01,957
If he's taken to you, he'll
definitely put in a good word with
His Lordship.

498
00:31:01,957 --> 00:31:04,397
I'd like to tell him to keep his
distance.

499
00:31:04,397 --> 00:31:06,517
Do you want to get your marching
orders, then?

500
00:31:07,997 --> 00:31:10,117
Why, what are you implying?

501
00:31:10,117 --> 00:31:12,157
Nothing unseemly, I hope?

502
00:31:12,157 --> 00:31:14,197
No.

503
00:31:14,197 --> 00:31:16,237
No, nothing like that.

504
00:31:16,237 --> 00:31:18,237
Good night.

505
00:31:38,395 --> 00:31:41,715
Mama, wake up! It's Sybil.

506
00:31:44,235 --> 00:31:45,875
(HYPERVENTILATING)

507
00:31:47,115 --> 00:31:48,835
(TRIES TO SPEAK)

508
00:31:49,675 --> 00:31:52,915
Can you hear me, darling? It's Tom.
I should be getting up.

509
00:31:52,915 --> 00:31:55,355
Darling, all you need to do is rest.

510
00:31:55,355 --> 00:31:56,915
(SHE CRIES OUT)

511
00:31:56,915 --> 00:31:58,555
Sybil?

512
00:31:58,555 --> 00:32:00,595
My head...!

513
00:32:00,595 --> 00:32:03,635
My head! Sybil? Let me bathe your
forehead.

514
00:32:03,635 --> 00:32:05,635
It hurts!

515
00:32:06,635 --> 00:32:08,275
What's happening?

516
00:32:08,275 --> 00:32:09,595
Oh, God.

517
00:32:09,595 --> 00:32:11,515
Oh, God!

518
00:32:11,515 --> 00:32:14,075
God, no, no! What the hell is
happening?

519
00:32:14,075 --> 00:32:16,075
Sybil? She can't hear me.

520
00:32:18,075 --> 00:32:21,115
Sybil? Sybil, it's Mary. Can you hear
me?

521
00:32:21,115 --> 00:32:22,755
It looks as if -

522
00:32:22,755 --> 00:32:25,395
It looks as if what? This is
eclampsia.

523
00:32:25,395 --> 00:32:26,835
Sybil?

524
00:32:26,835 --> 00:32:29,875
It cannot be, Sir Philip. You were
so sure.

525
00:32:30,795 --> 00:32:33,115
She can't hear me. This is
unbelievable.

526
00:32:33,115 --> 00:32:37,515
Darling... Somebody do something! The
human life is unpredictable. But you
were so sure!

527
00:32:37,515 --> 00:32:39,835
What can we do? Help her, help her,
please!

528
00:32:39,835 --> 00:32:41,675
(SYBIL HYPERVENTILATES)

529
00:32:41,675 --> 00:32:44,755
Oh, God, no! Dr Clarkson, shall we
take her to the hospital?

530
00:32:44,755 --> 00:32:47,875
There's nothing that can be done.
It's not possible, not these days!

531
00:32:47,875 --> 00:32:51,675
Once the seizures have started,
there's nothing to be done. You
don't agree, do you?

532
00:32:51,675 --> 00:32:55,315
Please don't leave me! Help her!
Help her, please!

533
00:32:55,315 --> 00:32:59,315
She can't breathe. Please, just
breathe.

534
00:32:59,315 --> 00:33:01,595
There has to be something worth
trying!

535
00:33:01,595 --> 00:33:03,715
Please! Come on, come on. Breathe,
love.

536
00:33:03,715 --> 00:33:07,355
Come on. Sybil? Listen, it's me, my
darling. All you need to do is
breathe.

537
00:33:07,355 --> 00:33:09,355
We've given her morphine, and
atropine.

538
00:33:10,155 --> 00:33:12,035
What's happening?

539
00:33:12,035 --> 00:33:13,515
Please breathe, love!

540
00:33:14,835 --> 00:33:16,075
Please!

541
00:33:16,075 --> 00:33:18,115
She can't breathe.

542
00:33:18,115 --> 00:33:20,115
(TOM SOBS)

543
00:33:22,755 --> 00:33:25,195
CORA: Oh, no, no!

544
00:33:25,195 --> 00:33:26,835
Please!

545
00:33:26,835 --> 00:33:28,835
Oh, no!

546
00:33:32,115 --> 00:33:35,115
TOM: Please, love.

547
00:33:37,235 --> 00:33:39,435
No, no! Please wake up.

548
00:33:40,435 --> 00:33:42,435
Please don't leave me.

549
00:33:43,355 --> 00:33:45,875
Please wake up, love. Please don't
leave me!

550
00:33:46,715 --> 00:33:48,755
Please don't leave me, love!

551
00:33:48,755 --> 00:33:50,435
(SHE GASPS)

552
00:33:50,435 --> 00:33:52,475
No! Oh, God!

553
00:33:52,475 --> 00:33:54,475
TOM: Please, love.

554
00:33:56,835 --> 00:33:58,835
(TOM SOBS)

555
00:34:02,715 --> 00:34:04,715
But this can't be.

556
00:34:05,715 --> 00:34:07,715
She's 24 years old.

557
00:34:09,275 --> 00:34:11,275
This cannot be.

558
00:34:15,915 --> 00:34:17,915
(CRIES OF BABY)

559
00:34:35,555 --> 00:34:37,875
Is there anything we should do, Mr
Carson?

560
00:34:38,675 --> 00:34:40,715
Carry on, Daisy.

561
00:34:42,635 --> 00:34:44,635
As we all must.

562
00:35:07,595 --> 00:35:09,635
Thomas?

563
00:35:13,395 --> 00:35:15,435
I don't know why I'm crying, really.

564
00:35:16,875 --> 00:35:18,995
She wouldn't have noticed if I'd
died.

565
00:35:20,035 --> 00:35:22,075
You don't mean that.

566
00:35:24,395 --> 00:35:27,195
No. No, I don't.

567
00:35:30,755 --> 00:35:33,755
In my life, I can tell you, not many
have been kind to me.

568
00:35:34,595 --> 00:35:36,635
She was one of the few.

569
00:35:43,875 --> 00:35:46,275
Oh... Don't mind me.

570
00:35:48,315 --> 00:35:50,795
The sweetest spirit under this roof
is gone,

571
00:35:52,435 --> 00:35:54,435
and I'm weeping myself.

572
00:36:05,795 --> 00:36:07,795
Are you all right, Mr Carson?

573
00:36:10,275 --> 00:36:12,275
I knew her all her life, you see.

574
00:36:14,955 --> 00:36:16,995
I've known her since she was born.

575
00:36:30,875 --> 00:36:32,875
We'll look after them.

576
00:36:34,995 --> 00:36:36,995
We'll look after them both.

577
00:36:38,795 --> 00:36:40,795
Don't you worry about that.

578
00:36:46,915 --> 00:36:48,915
It's time to go to bed, Mama.

579
00:36:49,795 --> 00:36:51,995
You'll need some rest to face
tomorrow.

580
00:36:54,315 --> 00:36:57,435
Not just yet. This is my chance to
say goodbye to my baby.

581
00:36:59,795 --> 00:37:01,795
You go.

582
00:37:02,835 --> 00:37:04,835
I'll be all right, I promise.

583
00:37:05,795 --> 00:37:09,275
I could stay, or would you prefer to
be alone?

584
00:37:10,035 --> 00:37:12,075
Alone, I think, but thank you.

585
00:37:14,155 --> 00:37:16,155
And Mary?

586
00:37:17,395 --> 00:37:20,915
Could you ask your father to sleep in
the dressing room tonight?

587
00:37:26,995 --> 00:37:28,995
Because you were my baby.

588
00:37:32,075 --> 00:37:34,395
You always will be. Always.

589
00:37:40,115 --> 00:37:42,115
My beauty, my baby.

590
00:37:59,355 --> 00:38:02,435
I've asked Carson to bring Anna
here, Mr Murray,

591
00:38:02,435 --> 00:38:07,515
but I don't think it'll be possible
for you to see Lord Grantham.

592
00:38:08,675 --> 00:38:11,435
Not today. I'm sure you understand.

593
00:38:11,435 --> 00:38:13,435
Of course.

594
00:38:15,395 --> 00:38:20,075
I should have guessed there was
something wrong when there was no car
at the station.

595
00:38:20,595 --> 00:38:22,635
What a dreadful, dreadful thing.

596
00:38:23,315 --> 00:38:25,355
(DOOR OPENS)

597
00:38:30,275 --> 00:38:32,315
I'll leave you to it.

598
00:38:32,315 --> 00:38:36,355
Mr Murray, I wonder if I might have
a word with you before you go?

599
00:38:36,355 --> 00:38:40,155
It's not the best day for it, but
there's no knowing when you might be
up here again.

600
00:38:40,155 --> 00:38:42,155
Of course, Mr Crawley.

601
00:38:51,835 --> 00:38:55,075
I am very sorry to trouble you on a
day like this, Mrs Bates.

602
00:38:55,075 --> 00:38:58,715
You weren't to know. None of us
could have known.

603
00:39:05,355 --> 00:39:07,475
The men from Grasby's have arrived.

604
00:39:09,715 --> 00:39:11,755
To take her away?

605
00:39:11,755 --> 00:39:13,795
Yes.

606
00:39:13,795 --> 00:39:15,795
And we must let them.

607
00:39:27,235 --> 00:39:29,275
Goodbye, my darling.

608
00:39:46,115 --> 00:39:50,635
She was the only person living who
always thought you and I were such
nice people.

609
00:39:51,995 --> 00:39:53,995
Oh, Mary...

610
00:39:57,115 --> 00:40:00,475
Do you think we might get along a
little better in the future?

611
00:40:00,475 --> 00:40:02,475
I doubt it.

612
00:40:04,555 --> 00:40:08,595
But since this is the last time we
three will all be together in this
life,

613
00:40:08,595 --> 00:40:11,675
let's love each other now, as sisters
should.

614
00:40:32,795 --> 00:40:36,595
You see, what I have discovered is
quite simple, Mr Murray.

615
00:40:36,595 --> 00:40:39,035
It's proof of my husband's
innocence.

616
00:40:39,035 --> 00:40:41,035
That seems a good place to start.

617
00:40:41,955 --> 00:40:44,995
Yes, but the key to his innocence

618
00:40:44,995 --> 00:40:47,355
depends on the word of a woman who
hates him,

619
00:40:47,795 --> 00:40:50,715
and may want him to stay in prison
whatever the truth.

620
00:40:52,195 --> 00:40:54,515
Why not tell me everything you know?

621
00:41:18,315 --> 00:41:20,435
Of course, this isn't the right
time,

622
00:41:20,435 --> 00:41:24,395
but you're here, and it's not a
subject for the telephone.

623
00:41:24,395 --> 00:41:27,955
No, but I must confess to you, Mr
Crawley, that

624
00:41:27,955 --> 00:41:32,955
even at this sad hour, your words are
music to my ears.

625
00:41:33,795 --> 00:41:36,955
Testing times are coming for these
estates, indeed.

626
00:41:36,955 --> 00:41:40,795
They've already arrived, and many
great families will go to the wall

627
00:41:40,795 --> 00:41:42,835
over the next few years.

628
00:41:42,835 --> 00:41:47,355
It's never been more vitally
important to maximise the assets of a
place like this,

629
00:41:47,355 --> 00:41:49,555
and run it with a solid business
head.

630
00:41:49,555 --> 00:41:51,555
What are you talking about?

631
00:41:52,355 --> 00:41:56,235
Mr Crawley and I were discussing the
management of the estate.

632
00:41:56,835 --> 00:41:59,995
He was outlining some interesting
plans for the future.

633
00:41:59,995 --> 00:42:03,555
And do you intend to involve my
father in these fascinating plans?

634
00:42:03,555 --> 00:42:05,555
Of course.

635
00:42:05,995 --> 00:42:11,355
Then I cannot think this a very
appropriate moment to be deciding
the destiny of Downton, Mr Murray,

636
00:42:11,355 --> 00:42:15,155
when my sister's body has just been
removed from the house,

637
00:42:15,155 --> 00:42:18,475
and my father is quite unable to see
or speak to anyone.

638
00:42:22,675 --> 00:42:26,235
I'm really only here to talk to Mrs
Bates about her new evidence.

639
00:42:26,235 --> 00:42:28,275
Naturally, if I'd known...

640
00:42:28,275 --> 00:42:30,315
No, no, that's quite different.

641
00:42:30,315 --> 00:42:34,675
None of us would wish to keep Bates
in prison for an hour longer than
necessary.

642
00:42:34,675 --> 00:42:37,395
Shall I fetch her? I've already seen
her.

643
00:42:38,395 --> 00:42:41,555
Now I'm on my way to York to visit
Bates,

644
00:42:41,555 --> 00:42:43,995
and learn what he has to say about
it.

645
00:42:43,995 --> 00:42:46,515
Then thank you so much for coming
all this way.

646
00:42:47,035 --> 00:42:51,075
Lady Mary, please tell your parents
how very sorry I am.

647
00:42:51,075 --> 00:42:53,075
Of course.

648
00:42:54,475 --> 00:42:56,475
Mr Murray is just leaving.

649
00:43:10,635 --> 00:43:14,075
I'm sorry, darling. Forgive me. I
wasn't thinking.

650
00:43:14,075 --> 00:43:16,115
It's just Murray was in the house...

651
00:43:16,115 --> 00:43:18,235
Papa has lost his youngest daughter.

652
00:43:18,235 --> 00:43:20,275
I think that's enough.

653
00:43:20,275 --> 00:43:23,555
Or does he have to lose control of
his estate on the same day?

654
00:43:32,115 --> 00:43:37,475
And the challenge is to get a
statement from Mrs Bartlett before
she realises its significance.

655
00:43:37,475 --> 00:43:39,475
That's it.

656
00:43:41,115 --> 00:43:43,315
I can't stop thinking about Lady
Sybil.

657
00:43:44,995 --> 00:43:47,795
A lovely young woman at the height
of her happiness.

658
00:43:48,675 --> 00:43:51,115
If I had any beliefs, that would
shake them.

659
00:43:51,115 --> 00:43:53,115
Make your way out now.

660
00:43:54,155 --> 00:43:56,155
I'll keep you informed, Mr Bates.

661
00:43:56,835 --> 00:43:58,875
I'll do my very best for you.

662
00:43:58,875 --> 00:44:00,875
Thank you, Mr Murray.

663
00:44:03,835 --> 00:44:05,835
I suppose that's his lawyer.

664
00:44:07,115 --> 00:44:09,155
Lord Grantham's lawyer, more like.

665
00:44:09,155 --> 00:44:11,915
I don't care if he's lawyer to the
Prince of Wales.

666
00:44:11,915 --> 00:44:15,115
He'll get a shock when he contacts
Mrs Audrey Bartlett.

667
00:44:27,035 --> 00:44:29,915
Oh, Carson. Good afternoon, my Lady.

668
00:44:32,115 --> 00:44:34,115
We've seen some troubles, you and I.

669
00:44:36,515 --> 00:44:40,235
Nothing worse than this. Nothing
COULD be worse than this, my Lady.

670
00:44:54,195 --> 00:44:56,195
(SHE GASPS)

671
00:45:12,035 --> 00:45:14,075
Mama.

672
00:45:14,075 --> 00:45:16,075
Oh, my dears.

673
00:45:18,955 --> 00:45:22,115
You'll be glad to know they've found
a nurse for the baby.

674
00:45:22,115 --> 00:45:24,915
She is already here. Good, good.

675
00:45:27,195 --> 00:45:29,235
Where's Tom?

676
00:45:29,235 --> 00:45:31,955
He's upstairs. I've asked if he wants
anything.

677
00:45:31,955 --> 00:45:33,955
He says no.

678
00:45:34,755 --> 00:45:37,675
He wants his wife back, but that's
what he can't have.

679
00:45:40,315 --> 00:45:43,795
I must write to Dr Clarkson and have
it sent down before dinner.

680
00:45:46,555 --> 00:45:49,675
Darling, there's no need for that.
I should. I want to.

681
00:45:49,675 --> 00:45:51,835
I have to apologise for our
behaviour.

682
00:45:51,835 --> 00:45:53,475
MARY: What?

683
00:45:53,475 --> 00:45:54,595
Why?

684
00:45:54,595 --> 00:45:57,955
Because if we'd listened to him,
Sybil might still be alive.

685
00:45:57,955 --> 00:46:01,435
But Sir Philip and your father knew
better, and now she's dead.

686
00:46:06,835 --> 00:46:08,835
Why... Why did she say that?

687
00:46:10,755 --> 00:46:12,755
Because there is some truth in it.

688
00:46:14,475 --> 00:46:17,555
My dear, when tragedies strike,

689
00:46:17,555 --> 00:46:19,595
we try to find someone to blame.

690
00:46:19,595 --> 00:46:24,035
In the absence of a suitable
candidate, we usually blame
ourselves.

691
00:46:25,115 --> 00:46:28,515
You are not to blame. No-one is to
blame.

692
00:46:30,195 --> 00:46:36,035
Our darling Sybil has died during
childbirth,

693
00:46:37,595 --> 00:46:39,635
like too many women before her,

694
00:46:39,635 --> 00:46:41,635
and all we can do now...

695
00:46:43,475 --> 00:46:45,475
..is cherish her memory,

696
00:46:45,915 --> 00:46:47,915
and her child.

697
00:46:49,315 --> 00:46:51,315
Nevertheless, there is truth in it.

698
00:47:07,115 --> 00:47:09,235
I thought I might move back in here
tonight.

699
00:47:09,235 --> 00:47:11,595
I think I'd rather sleep alone for a
while.

700
00:47:11,595 --> 00:47:14,275
We should think about the
christening. She'll be Catholic.

701
00:47:14,275 --> 00:47:16,595
She's Irish, and will be Catholic
like her father.

702
00:47:16,595 --> 00:47:19,755
What were you doing at Crawley House
this morning? Who says I was there?

703
00:47:19,755 --> 00:47:22,515
I saw you coming out. Oh, I see.

704
00:47:22,515 --> 00:47:26,555
You look very nice this morning.
Don't flirt with me, Robert, not
now.

705
00:47:26,555 --> 00:47:28,275
What do we do next?

706
00:47:28,275 --> 00:47:30,395
ANNA: You mustn't do anything
stupid.

707
00:47:30,395 --> 00:47:32,115
Promise me.

708
00:47:32,115 --> 00:47:34,875
You won't win over the Christening.
Not if you're against me.

709
00:47:34,875 --> 00:47:37,155
I'm never against you, but you've
lost on this one.

710
00:47:37,155 --> 00:47:39,155
itfc subtitles

